Chapter: AppPersist
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256
  cites Foreword chapter Hamt
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter FactLog
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  grounds Device.Block

 Shared persistence helpers for application data. Extends DiskFacts
 with multi-sector fact writes and kind-based scanning. Apps assign
 kind integers 20+ to avoid collision with system kinds (0-14).

 Kind allocation:
   20 = Browser history
   21 = Browser trust decisions
   22 = Diagram data
   23 = Secrets vault metadata
   24 = Secret entries
   25 = FileShare manifests
   26 = Revocation records
   27 = Parental control settings
   30 = Repository definitions (source file hashes)
   31 = Repository proofs (verification evidence)
   32 = Repository vouches (trust endorsements)
   33 = Repository policies (access rules)
   34 = Repository capabilities (granted permissions)
   35 = Repository annotations
   36 = Repository proposals
   37 = Repository verdicts
   38 = Repository discussion posts
   39 = Repository tombstones (a path retired)
   40 = Repository index snapshot (a materialized WorkIndex checkpoint)
   41 = Repository archive base (a signed, compressed range of the log)

Section: The Layout Lives In FactLog

 This chapter used to carry its own copy of the fact log's shape: a header size
 of 78, a sector size of 512, its own sector-count formula, a writer that
 chained each field off the previous one's return value, and four readers that
 reached into a sector buffer at bare 32, 66 and 74. FactLog exists precisely so
 that shape has one definition, and DiskFacts was moved onto it -- but this
 chapter was left behind, which made FactLog's guarantee a half-truth. A change
 to the layout that DiskFacts followed and AppPersist did not is a divergence
 nothing would catch until a fact read back as garbage.

 So every size, offset and field read here now asks FactLog. There is nothing
 left in this file that knows what a fact header looks like.

Section: Copy Helpers

  app-copy-sector : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  app-copy-sector (src) (dst) (dst-off) (i) =
    if i >= fl-sector-size then 0
    else let w = poke-byte dst (dst-off + i) (peek-byte src i)
    in app-copy-sector src dst dst-off (i + 1)

Section: Sectors Are Relative To The Store

 Every sector number below is relative to the store's base LBA, exactly as
 in DiskFacts, and goes through that chapter's fact-read-at / fact-write-at.
 This chapter used to name absolute sectors, which on a partitioned disk is
 the partition table. A store carries the base it was opened against, so
 the base comes off the store rather than being resolved here.

  app-load-sectors : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  app-load-sectors (base) (start) (count) (dst) (i) =
    if i >= count then 0
    else let src = fact-read-at base (start + i)
    in let c = app-copy-sector src dst (i * fl-sector-size) 0
    in app-load-sectors base start count dst (i + 1)

Section: Write

 DiskFacts packs an entry whose kind is a FactKind; the kinds this chapter hands
 out are plain integers past that variant's range, so the packing is here rather
 than borrowed. What is NOT here is the layout: each field goes where FactLog
 says it goes.

 The old writer chained instead -- hash returns 32, the kind write returns 34,
 step over the gap with a bare + 32, and so on -- so the offsets were reachable
 only by adding the widths up in your head, and the 32-byte gap between the kind
 and the timestamp was a magic number in the middle of an expression. A writer
 that computes what a reader looks up is the same divergence with one side
 hidden. The gap is still there and still zero: the buffer is zeroed before
 anything is packed into it, and nothing writes those bytes.

  app-pack-fact : Integer, Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  app-pack-fact (buf) (kind-int) (content) (timestamp) =
    let hw = sha256 (text-to-bytes content)
    in let h = pack-sha256-bytes buf 0 hw
    in let k = pack-u16-le buf fl-off-kind kind-int
    in let t = pack-u64-le buf fl-off-timestamp timestamp
    in let c = pack-u32-le buf fl-off-content-len (text-length content)
    in pack-text buf fl-header-size content

 The two refusals are DiskFacts', for DiskFacts' reasons: a store whose
 base is negative sits on a disk that carries a partition table and no
 fact store, and a write that would run past the region's limit would
 land in whatever partition follows. Both answer the store UNCHANGED, so
 a caller watching disk-fact-count sees it fail to rise. This writer had
 neither guard and wrote straight through both.

  app-write-fact : DiskFactStore, Integer, Text, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  app-write-fact (store) (kind-int) (content) (timestamp) =
    let sb = store.superblock
    in let clen = text-length content
    in let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in if disk-usable store == False then store
    else if region-admits (store.limit) (sb.log-head + nsec) == False then store
    else let buf = alloc-bytes (nsec * fl-sector-size)
    in let z = zero-bytes buf 0 (nsec * fl-sector-size)
    in let packed = app-pack-fact buf kind-int content timestamp
    in let w = write-fact-sectors (store.base) buf (sb.log-head) nsec 0
    in DiskFactStore {
      superblock = Superblock {
        log-head = sb.log-head + nsec,
        fact-count = sb.fact-count + 1,
        index-gen = sb.index-gen
      },
      mem = store.mem,
      base = store.base,
      limit = store.limit
    }

  app-write-and-checkpoint : DiskFactStore, Integer, Text, Integer -> DiskFactStore
  app-write-and-checkpoint (store) (kind-int) (content) (timestamp) =
    disk-checkpoint (app-write-fact store kind-int content timestamp)

Section: Read Content

  app-read-content : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  app-read-content (base) (start) (clen) =
    let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in let buf = alloc-bytes (nsec * fl-sector-size)
    in let loaded = app-load-sectors base start nsec buf 0
    in unpack-text buf fl-header-size clen

Section: Scan for Latest Fact of a Given Kind

  app-scan-last : DiskFactStore, Integer -> Maybe Text
  app-scan-last (store) (kind) =
    app-scan-loop (store.base) kind fact-log-start (store.superblock.log-head) None

  app-scan-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Maybe Text -> Maybe Text
  app-scan-loop (base) (kind) (sector) (end-sec) (found) =
    if sector >= end-sec then found
    else let buf = fact-read-at base sector
    in let k = fl-entry-kind buf
    in let clen = fl-entry-content-len buf
    in if clen == 0 then app-scan-loop base kind (sector + 1) end-sec found
    else let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in if k == kind then
      let txt = app-read-content base sector clen
      in app-scan-loop base kind (sector + nsec) end-sec (Just txt)
    else app-scan-loop base kind (sector + nsec) end-sec found

Section: Scan for All Facts of a Given Kind

  app-scan-all : DiskFactStore, Integer -> List Text
  app-scan-all (store) (kind) =
    app-scan-all-loop (store.base) kind fact-log-start (store.superblock.log-head) []

  app-scan-all-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  app-scan-all-loop (base) (kind) (sector) (end-sec) (acc) =
    if sector >= end-sec then acc
    else let buf = fact-read-at base sector
    in let k = fl-entry-kind buf
    in let clen = fl-entry-content-len buf
    in if clen == 0 then app-scan-all-loop base kind (sector + 1) end-sec acc
    else let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in if k == kind then
      let txt = app-read-content base sector clen
      in app-scan-all-loop base kind (sector + nsec) end-sec (list-push acc txt)
    else app-scan-all-loop base kind (sector + nsec) end-sec acc

Section: Scan Two Kinds In Log Order

 Some readers need more than one kind, in the order the log wrote them --
 a repository tree, for one, reads source definitions and their tombstones
 together, because which one is later for a given path is the whole
 question. This walks the log once and yields each matching entry tagged
 with its kind, in log order.

  KindText = record {
    kt-kind : Integer,
    kt-text : Text
  }

  app-scan-two-kinds : DiskFactStore, Integer, Integer -> List KindText
  app-scan-two-kinds (store) (kind-a) (kind-b) =
    app-scan-two-loop (store.base) kind-a kind-b fact-log-start (store.superblock.log-head) []

 The same walk, but from a given sector rather than the start of the log. An
 index snapshot covers the log up to a sector; rebuilding on top of it needs
 only the entries written after that point, so a fast rebuild scans the tail
 instead of the whole log. The start sector is a fact boundary (it was the
 log-head captured when the snapshot was taken), so the walk lands cleanly on
 the first entry that the snapshot did not already fold in.

  app-scan-two-kinds-from : DiskFactStore, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List KindText
  app-scan-two-kinds-from (store) (start) (kind-a) (kind-b) =
    app-scan-two-loop (store.base) kind-a kind-b start (store.superblock.log-head) []

  app-scan-two-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List KindText -> List KindText
  app-scan-two-loop (base) (kind-a) (kind-b) (sector) (end-sec) (acc) =
    if sector >= end-sec then acc
    else let buf = fact-read-at base sector
    in let k = fl-entry-kind buf
    in let clen = fl-entry-content-len buf
    in if clen == 0 then app-scan-two-loop base kind-a kind-b (sector + 1) end-sec acc
    else let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in if (k == kind-a) | (k == kind-b) then
      let txt = app-read-content base sector clen
      in app-scan-two-loop base kind-a kind-b (sector + nsec) end-sec (list-push acc (KindText { kt-kind = k, kt-text = txt }))
    else app-scan-two-loop base kind-a kind-b (sector + nsec) end-sec acc

Section: Log Compaction

 Compaction used to keep ONE FACT PER KIND. It walked the log and dropped any
 entry whose kind it had already seen, so a store with five hundred facts of
 the same kind compacted down to one and the other four hundred and ninety-nine
 were gone.

 That is the right answer for a kind that holds a single current value -- the
 browser's history blob is rewritten whole each time, and only the newest is
 worth keeping -- and it is a catastrophe for a kind that holds a collection.
 Kind 24 is secret ENTRIES. Kind 25 is FileShare manifests. Kind 26 is
 revocation records. Kind 30 is every source definition in the repository. All
 of them are collections, and compacting any of them would have destroyed all
 but one member.

 The log cannot tell the difference. Nothing on the disk says that fact B
 supersedes fact A; a fact is a kind, a timestamp, a hash and some bytes.
 Guessing the relation from the kind is how you lose a repository, and the kind
 space already contains counterexamples.

 So the rule is the conservative one, and it is the only one the data supports:
 a compactor that cannot prove two facts are the same must keep them both.
 Identity for a content-addressed fact is its content hash, which is already in
 the entry header -- so two entries collapse only when they are the same kind
 AND the same hash, which is to say the same bytes written twice. Nothing else
 is ever dropped.

 The consequence is honest and worth stating: compaction no longer reclaims
 space from a superseded singleton, because the disk holds no evidence that it
 was superseded, and the conservative rule is the correct trade. Reclaiming
 space is what the archive base is for -- a signed, compressed copy of a range
 of the log, recorded in the live log as the base (Works chapter FactArchive) --
 and it does not guess at supersession either: it moves history to another tier
 rather than deciding that any of it is redundant.

 The old walk had a second defect. It collected survivors newest-first and
 handed them straight to compact-rewrite, so a compacted log came out REVERSED
 -- and app-scan-last, which trusts log order to mean recency, would then have
 returned the OLDEST fact of a kind. Survivors are restored to log order here.

  CompactEntry = record {
    ce-kind : Integer,
    ce-sector : Integer,
    ce-clen : Integer,
    ce-timestamp : Integer,
    ce-hash : Text
  }

  compact-scan : Integer, Integer, Integer, List CompactEntry -> List CompactEntry
  compact-scan (base) (sector) (end-sec) (acc) =
    if sector >= end-sec then acc
    else let buf = fact-read-at base sector
    in let k = fl-entry-kind buf
    in let clen = fl-entry-content-len buf
    in if clen == 0 then compact-scan base (sector + 1) end-sec acc
    else let nsec = fl-sectors-for clen
    in let ts = fl-entry-timestamp buf
    in let h = unpack-sha256-hex buf 0
    in let entry = CompactEntry { ce-kind = k, ce-sector = sector, ce-clen = clen, ce-timestamp = ts, ce-hash = h }
    in compact-scan base (sector + nsec) end-sec (list-push acc entry)

  compact-identity : CompactEntry -> Text
  compact-identity (e) = show (e.ce-kind) & ":" & (e.ce-hash)

  compact-dedup : List CompactEntry -> List CompactEntry
  compact-dedup (entries) =
    let survivors = compact-dedup-loop entries (list-length entries - 1) hamt-empty []
    in compact-reverse survivors (list-length survivors - 1) []

  compact-dedup-loop : List CompactEntry, Integer, HamtMap Boolean, List CompactEntry -> List CompactEntry
  compact-dedup-loop (entries) (i) (seen) (kept) =
    if i < 0 then kept
    else let e = list-at entries i
    in let key = compact-identity e
    in when hamt-get seen key
      is Just (b) -> compact-dedup-loop entries (i - 1) seen kept
      is None -> compact-dedup-loop entries (i - 1) (hamt-set seen key True) (list-push kept e)

  compact-reverse : List CompactEntry, Integer, List CompactEntry -> List CompactEntry
  compact-reverse (xs) (i) (acc) =
    if i < 0 then acc
    else compact-reverse xs (i - 1) (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

  compact-rewrite : Integer, List CompactEntry, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  compact-rewrite (base) (kept) (write-sector) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then write-sector
    else let e = list-at kept i
    in let nsec = fl-sectors-for (e.ce-clen)
    in let src-buf = alloc-bytes (nsec * fl-sector-size)
    in let loaded = app-load-sectors base (e.ce-sector) nsec src-buf 0
    in let written = write-fact-sectors base src-buf write-sector nsec 0
    in compact-rewrite base kept (write-sector + nsec) (i + 1) len

  disk-compact : DiskFactStore -> DiskFactStore
  disk-compact (store) =
    let sb = store.superblock
    in if disk-usable store == False then store
    else if sb.fact-count <= 1 then store
    else let entries = compact-scan (store.base) fact-log-start (sb.log-head) []
    in let kept = compact-dedup entries
    in let kept-count = list-length kept
    in if kept-count >= sb.fact-count then store
    else let new-head = compact-rewrite (store.base) kept fact-log-start 0 kept-count
    in let new-sb = Superblock {
        log-head = new-head,
        fact-count = kept-count,
        index-gen = sb.index-gen + 1
      }
    in let written = write-superblock (store.base) new-sb
    in DiskFactStore {
        superblock = new-sb,
        mem = store.mem,
        base = store.base,
        limit = store.limit
      }
